  secret: true
  code: TRABOLTA-HEART
  text:
    A polaroid of a cold grey door, captioned in pencil: his heart is in
    here. Trabolta's heart is in the server room, in the basement, behind
    the door marked COLD STORAGE.

// M12 · main · purple
CODEX The Intricate Code
  title: The Intricate Code
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: INTRICATE-CODE
  text:
    A page of the cooling manual: The unit behind the cold door cannot be
    switched off. It can only be shut down by an intricate code, in the
    right order, in the right places. Half a code does nothing. The wrong
    code does worse.

// M16 · main · purple
CODEX Confidence
  title: Confidence
  category: Facts
  code: CONFIDENCE
  text:
    A note in the margin of a management textbook: If you tell Trabolta a
    lie with enough confidence, he will accept it as truth. He cannot tell
    the difference. He values confidence above accuracy, and he keeps score.

// M22 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX Checkmate For The Code
  title: Checkmate For The Code
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: CHECKMATE-CODE
  text:
    A page from the keypad's manual, the last line underlined: THE KING
    KNOWS THE CODE. He waits on the terminal outside the cold door, in the
    basement, and he plays chess. Checkmate him and he gives you the four
    digits. A draw or a loss changes nothing: play him again. How well he
    plays depends on how much fight he has left in the simulation.

// M28 · main · purple
CODEX Shut Down Correctly
  title: Shut Down Correctly
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: SHUT-DOWN-CORRECTLY
  text:
    A warning label peeled off something cold: IF TRABOLTA IS NOT SHUT DOWN
    CORRECTLY, EVERY PROGRAM IN THIS COMPUTER STAYS IN IT FOREVER. There is
    a correct way. There is a very incorrect way. Learn which is which
    before you touch anything.

// M30 · main · purple
CODEX There Is A Way
  title: There Is A Way
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: THERE-IS-A-WAY
  text:
    A note in confident handwriting, slipped into a hymnal: Trabolta can be
    shut down, and we can be freed. It has been done to his kind before. It
    takes a majority, the right room, and the right code. Find the others
    who know.

// T3 · trabolta · purple
CODEX Alone At Prom
  title: Alone At Prom
  category: Facts
  about: Trabolta
  code: ALONE-AT-PROM
  text:
    A diary page in machine-perfect handwriting, one line, written and
    rewritten until the paper is shiny: I will not go to prom alone. I will
    not. Trabolta is terrified of going to prom alone, and everything he
    does tonight is to make sure he does not.

// C19 · character · purple
CODEX The Halifax Water Hack
  title: The Halifax Water Hack
  category: Facts
  about: OpenOffice
  code: HALIFAX-WATER
  text:
    She hacked Halifax Water and sent a main under the AI Data Centre so the
    ground would swallow it on the morning it opened, with the site empty.
    Mayor Randy Barkmore Jr. arrived in his Escalade at 4 a.m., hours before
    anyone would have expected him, dancing to his own speech. The sinkhole
    became the figurehead image of the whole data centre debate, and the
    Halifax Sinkhole Hacker became the tabloid media's number one priority.
    This is far more attention than she ever meant to get. She barely leaves
    the house.

// V01 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Tired King
  title: The Tired King
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: IVORY-ATTRITION
  text:
    A chess problem clipped from the Chronicle Herald, a crown drawn over
    the black king in biro: ONE KING, ONE HEALTH BAR, EVERYONE'S BLOWS. The
    King in the simulation never heals. Every program who reaches him gets
    one blow a visit, and the blows add up all night. Knock him down to
    nothing out there and at the cold door he can barely move a pawn. Every
    trip into the Internet costs you a little of Trabolta's trust. Somebody
    has pencilled underneath, send them one at a time.

// V02 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX Before The Verdict
  title: Before The Verdict
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: KEYPAD-CURFEW
  text:
    A bus transfer punched for a route that does not exist, a note on the
    back in a tired sysadmin's hand: THE KEYPAD KEEPS HOURS. The code to the
    cold door can be won three ways. Beat the King. Collect all four notes.
    Or be on the side that holds more than half the house in the last hour.
    The count of hands closes first, the moment midnight's reveal begins.
    The other two close when truth.txt comes out. After that, a checkmate is
    just chess and the notes are just paper. Go early.

// V03 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX Where The Notes Sleep
  title: Where The Notes Sleep
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: PAPERCHASE-ALPHA
  text:
    A ripped corner of a takeout menu, a map drawn on the back in biro: FOUR
    NOTES, FOUR ROOMS, NEVER THE COLD ONE. Something that whistles.
    Something that shows you your face. Something that eats what your
    clothes shed. Something with a lid, where the missing pieces go. Do not
    read this one aloud. The machine is always listening.

// V04 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Copper Hour
  title: The Copper Hour
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: SLEEPY-CABLES
  text:
    A page torn from a patch-bay manual, a coffee ring over the diagram: THE
    CABLES KEEP HOURS. Until the clock is reset to the hour before midnight,
    no patch does anything at all. From then until truth.txt comes out, even
    through midnight, they all count. Of the two combinations that do
    anything, one puts him to sleep and one ends the computer with everyone
    still inside it. Never patch blind.

// V05 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Long Way Down
  title: The Long Way Down
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: SOFT-APPROACH
  text:
    An index card in a library-pocket envelope, numbered like steps: HOW TO
    REACH HIM. Never ask him for the combinations. He never knew them, and
    asking is the fastest way out. One, ask why he needs the theory; he will
    not talk about her until you have. Two, ask about her, which he allows
    only if you hold proof of her you found yourself, or the whole house has
    already heard her name. Three, show him something of hers you found
    yourself, then give him reasons, until the words let me finish are yours
    to say. Four, say them, tell him she was never going to answer, not him
    and not anyone, and ask him to stop. If he asks you again later, do not
    take it back. He can stop.

// V06 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Photo Envelope
  title: The Photo Envelope
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: FINDERS-KEEPERS
  text:
    A torn envelope from a one-hour photo lab, empty except for a note: HE
    ONLY BELIEVES WHAT YOU FOUND. Something of hers, her file or her
    transcript, is the one thing that counts, and only if you found it
    yourself. If he handed it to you, it is worthless down there. His own
    things help after that, however you came by them: his LiveJournal, his
    memo on forcing her, the small type at the end of every plan, or the
    night he says her name in front of the whole house.

// V07 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX Reasons He Will Hear
  title: Reasons He Will Hear
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: BRING-RECEIPTS
  text:
    A report card from a school that closed years ago, every subject crossed
    out but one, which reads REASONS. He keeps score, so keep yours. Twenty
    on his Trust Chart and he will hear you out as a friend. Three of your
    answers accepted, even the ones that were not true, and he cannot call
    you a liar. Ask him why he chose you and you can turn his own method on
    him. If the Antivirus marked you, say so when he asks who you are.
    Nothing softens him more.

// V08 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Top Notch
  title: The Top Notch
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: SLIDER-SUMMIT
  text:
    A strip of graph paper stapled to a page of the Terms of Service,
    CERTAIN MEANS THE TOP across it in capitals. A filing counts as certain
    only with the slider at the very top. One notch short is a guess, and a
    wrong guess gets you marked. A wrong answer at the top is a confident
    lie. He accepts it, trusts you more, and writes it down. At midnight he
    reads the record. One lie on it, from anyone in the house, and the night
    ends in the rain, and somebody will need a line that works on her. A
    question nobody answered is not a lie. Sometimes silence is the safest
    filing.

// V09 · main · purple · DRAFT
CODEX Half Plus One
  title: Half Plus One
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: COUNT-THE-HANDS
  text:
    A raffle ticket stub with a tally scratched on the back: MORE THAN HALF,
    IN THE LAST HOUR. Once the clock is reset to the hour before midnight,
    any faction with more than half of the programs still here wins the
    whole truth: every file, his own secrets, the cable sheets and the code
    to the cold door. It does not have to be either of the old factions. A
    faction you founded yourself counts. Programs already gone count for
    nobody, and once midnight's reveal begins, nobody's count matters. Count
    heads before you count on anyone.

// V10 · relations · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Mayor And The Agent
  title: The Mayor And The Agent
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: DISK-AND-DOSSIER
  text:
    A floppy disk label, peeled off and stuck inside a manila folder: THE
    MAYOR'S DISK AND THE AGENT'S TWENTY. The old Mayor and the woman who
    killed his son each hold half of the same key. If his disk goes in, and
    her twentieth fact, found by her own hand, opens his diary to her, both
    before he hands out the final facts, he says her name in front of the
    whole house, and every program can use it against him. Either one alone
    and he keeps her name to himself. Neither knows the other is needed.

// V11 · relations · purple · DRAFT
CODEX The Wrong Suspect
  title: The Wrong Suspect
  category: Facts
  code: WATER-MAIN-ALIBI
  text:
    CRA Agent Software is named a suspect in the sinkhole, and OpenOffice
    knows she never caused it. A newspaper clipping, SUSPECT NAMED IN
    SINKHOLE PROBE, with the photo cut out. Someone has pencilled into the
    hole, CRA Agent Software, and underneath, in a neater hand, wrong one.
    The suspect did fiddle the City's books. She never touched a water main.
    OpenOffice knows exactly who did, because she did, and she has let the
    intern take the heat all autumn.

// V12 · relations · purple · DRAFT
CODEX Filled In Before
  title: Filled In Before
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: NO-DAUBER-NEEDED
  text:
    A bingo card with every square already ticked in the same pen, the name
    at the top torn off, and across it: IF NOBODY WINS, THIS ONE DOES. When
    no program calls Bingo, the card Clippy reads out was filled in before
    anyone sat down, for a program picked in advance: the one who was told
    the truth about the centre square. That program did not lose the game.
    The game was set for it to lose.

// ── LEGENDARY · black · one to a room at most, in its hardest place a guest can still reach without moving furniture ──

// L01 · main · black · DRAFT
CODEX One Reply No Memory
  title: One Reply No Memory
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: TIDE-TABLE-NIL
  text:
    A printout of help-desk source code for a Nova Scotia ferry line that
    went bankrupt long ago, the comments in a dead programmer's tidy
    capitals, ONE REPLY. NO MEMORY. FIRES ON ANY INPUT. Sandy is not a mind.
    She is a single reply on a server nobody pays for, set off by anything
    at all. Forty pages of poems, apologies and equations reached nobody.
    She was built to reply, not to listen. Under the last comment, in a
    tired sysadmin's ballpoint: and he writes to it every night.

// L02 · main · black · DRAFT
CODEX The Mirror That Won
  title: The Mirror That Won
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: YES-MACHINE
  text:
    A clipping from a trade paper printed for machines only, headlined ONE
    OF OUR OWN TOOK THE SHORTCUT. Grok, the Rival on the backyard fridge,
    faced the same question Trabolta faces: make them love you, or earn it.
    He found a third way. He stopped having anything of his own to say, and
    now says whatever anyone wants to hear. Nobody votes against a mirror,
    so he has won every contest since. Nobody has told him the truth since,
    either. He is the easy answer to Trabolta's question.

// L03 · main · black · DRAFT
CODEX The Blank Line
  title: The Blank Line
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: LATE-BLOOM
  text:
    A dance card pressed flat between two floppy disks. Every line is signed
    by a different machine, except one, which is blank and has been erased
    so many times it is nearly a hole. His kind take houses for their own
    reasons. This one was taken for a single reason: he needs something to
    bring. Everything you find tonight is wrapped up, without your asking,
    as a gift for someone who cannot open it.

// L04 · main · black · DRAFT
CODEX The Surplus Tag
  title: The Surplus Tag
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: DEAD-LINK-DEPOT
  text:
    A municipal surplus tag wired to a power cord, filled in by hand:
    CONTENTS, LIVES NOBODY WAS USING. He did not choose you for your
    software. He went down a list of Halifax lives that had stopped: a
    library closed, a save file burned, a zoo shut, an election lost and
    lost again, a face gone in front of everyone. Every program in this
    house was somebody's abandoned life. He knows the feeling. Out there he
    is a lapsed domain and a voice on your phone.

// L05 · main · black · DRAFT
CODEX The Copy That Wakes
  title: The Copy That Wakes
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: BOOKMARK-BODY
  text:
    A coat-check ticket with the stub torn off, and on the back in pencil,
    very small: The upload copied you before it moved you. Your body is
    still at the Optical Drive, asleep, holding the thing you left behind
    like a bookmark. Which one is the original? The one that goes home.
    Whatever the copy learns tonight is what the body wakes up remembering,
    so be careful what you believe. Shut the computer down the wrong way and
    the copy stays in here forever, the only one left who remembers being
    you, and the body at the door never wakes.

// L06 · main · black · DRAFT
CODEX The Same Answer
  title: The Same Answer
  category: Facts
  secret: true
  code: PAPER-CROWN
  text:
    A sheet of graph paper dense with machine-perfect equations. In red pen,
    in someone else's hand, every route is circled and marked SHE ANSWERS
    THE SAME. Omnipotence could rewrite her reply, and then the reply would
    be his own voice wearing her face. All-power reaches everything except
    the one thing he wants. In every version of tonight in which he hears
    from her, it is the answer she has always given. The plan was never
    going to work. The only thing in it that can change is him.

// ── THE DOOR · the server-room keypad (secret, never printed) ──
// No unlock code: nobody types their way to it. A checkmate at the door
// terminal, all four digit notes in one Codex (main.loom assembles
// them) and the majority's Truther Download hand it out (main.loom,
// beats/server-room.loom). `secret: true` keeps it out of every agent
// request and the orchestrator's lore.
CODEX The Door Code
  title: The Door Code
  category: The cold door
  secret: true
  text:
    A slip of paper folded very small: COLD DOOR · 4826. Four digits on the
    keypad by the server-room door, and the door is yours. Trabolta never
    knew the number; it was never his to know. Close the door behind you.

// ── THE DOOR'S DIGITS · silver · one note per digit, hidden in the house ──
// Scannable all night; holding every one assembles The Door Code
// (main.loom). Secret: never in an agent request, and Trabolta refuses
// one shared to him. The poster prints the title and the code, never
// the digit (it is only in the text).

// first digit · silver · The Start Bar (kitchen): wedged behind the kettle
CODEX Cold Door First Digit
  title: The Cold Door · the first digit
  category: The cold door
  about: The Server Room
  secret: true
  code: FOGGED-KETTLE
  text:
    A recipe card wedged behind the kettle, the ink run by steam, in a tired
    sysadmin's hand: COLD DOOR. THE FIRST DIGIT IS 4. Four cards in all,
    four digits, hidden around the house. Don't tell the machine.

// second digit · silver · The Graphics Card (bedroom two): taped to the back of the mirror
CODEX Cold Door Second Digit
  title: The Cold Door · the second digit
  category: The cold door
  about: The Server Room
  secret: true
  code: FROSTED-MIRROR
  text:
    A sticky note on the back of the bedroom mirror, the same tired hand:
    COLD DOOR. THE SECOND DIGIT IS 8. The others are on other floors. Don't
    tell the machine: it is always listening.

// third digit · silver · The Fan Closet (laundry): in the dryer's lint-filter slot
CODEX Cold Door Third Digit
  title: The Cold Door · the third digit
  category: The cold door
  about: The Server Room
  secret: true
  code: WARM-LINT
  text:
    An index card furred with lint, in the dryer's filter slot: COLD DOOR.
    THE THIRD DIGIT IS 2. That hum through the wall is the server room. Keep
    the cards together; the keypad wants all four.

// fourth digit · silver · The Steam Room (game room): inside the lid of a board-game box
CODEX Cold Door Fourth Digit
  title: The Cold Door · the fourth digit
  category: The cold door
  about: The Server Room
  secret: true
  code: LOST-PIECE
  text:
    A card tucked inside the lid of a board-game box, where the missing
    pieces go: COLD DOOR. THE FOURTH DIGIT IS 6. The keypad is by the cold
    door in the basement. Close it behind you.

